{
 "id": "2023-lam-soundscape",
 "name": "Soundscape Listening Test (SATP)",
 "description": "Headphone listening test of the Soundscape Attributes Translation Project (SATP), Stage 2. Participants hear the 27 standard 30-second binaural recordings of London streets, squares, parks and canals in random order and, after hearing each recording at least once (replays allowed), rate the eight ISO/TS 12913-2 perceived affective qualities (pleasant, chaotic, vibrant, uneventful, calm, annoying, eventful, monotonous) on 0–100 sliders from strongly disagree to strongly agree, starting at 50. ISOPleasant and ISOEventful follow ISO/TS 12913-3. The recordings and 17,000+ ratings in 19 languages are open on Zenodo (CC BY 4.0, doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6914433), so a new sample or language can be compared recording by recording. Protocol as described in Lam et al. (2023), Applied Acoustics 214: 109675 (Standard Malay, Singapore vs Malaysia) and the SATP dataset README. Faithful replication: the original SATP recordings. Differences from the original study: (1) online instead of a lab with calibrated headphones (open-circuit-voltage calibration is not possible in a browser); participants set their volume once with an 8-second level-check clip (the first 8 s of recording E11b) and report what they listen with; (2) English attribute words only (the SATP protocol is run in each target language; translate the eight words for another language); (3) recordings are served as 192 kbps MP3 converted from the 24-bit WAV files with levels unchanged.",
